## Shared working tree — the coordination lane

**This tree is shared, and not only with you.** Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode,
WorkBuddy and GLM all edit these same files at the same time. A peer's
half-finished edit that you overwrite is work that cannot be recovered from any
transcript. The repo keeps a **Live Pair-Coding Coordination Ledger** at
`.ai-workflow/coordination/` — gitignored, same-machine files, so every seat sees
the others instantly with zero git churn.

**Before your first edit, run:**

```bash
node scripts/lane-at-root.mjs orientation
```

That is the complete, uncapped discovery command: it lists **every** lane and
**every** parsed claim, and its exit code says whether the answer can be trusted
as clearance — `0` complete, `2` valid but INCOMPLETE, `1` unproducible. If it is
not `0`, a target you cannot see may be claimed; that is not the same as free.
Then read `.ai-workflow/coordination/review-queue.md` for open review requests.

`node scripts/lane.mjs digest` is the cheap **startup summary** — use it to
orient. It is deliberately **capped**: lock paths per seat are capped at 5 and
live lanes at 6, and its `me:` line prints `agent@slug`, **not a filepath**. An
agent asking "is my file locked?" through `digest` can be shown a truncated list
and wrongly conclude the file is free. **`digest` is not clearance.**

**Do not enumerate lane files by name.** Seats are named per-session —
`<agent>--<worktree-slug>[-s<session-hash>].lane.md` — so a hardcoded list
silently misses live seats. Measured miss, 2026-09-20: the documented list named
two seats; the seat actually holding a lock on
`backend/routes/bridge/bridgeIngestRoutes.mjs` was a third name the list never
mentioned. An agent following the docs verbatim would have read both named files,
seen no conflict, and edited a locked file. Concrete filenames here illustrate the
scheme — they are not the way to find seats. Discover them with the command.

**For the whole session:**

- **Read-before-edit** — run `orientation` before editing any file. If your target
  is under any seat's `🔒 EDITING NOW`, do not edit it. Pick another file, append a
  request to `review-queue.md`, or ask Sean.
- **Claim/release** — overwrite **your own** lane file. Its exact path comes from
  `node scripts/lane.mjs whoami` or the `self.laneFile` field of complete
  discovery — **never** reconstructed from a display name. Write status, the exact
  files under `🔒 EDITING NOW`, and an ISO `Updated:` stamp when you start a slice;
  clear it when done. **Never write another seat's lane file.**
- **Commit safety** — never `git add -A` while another seat holds locks; stage
  explicit paths.
- **Staleness — a warning, never a release.** A lane stamped **>30 min** ago and
  still `in-progress` has **suspect** claims: say so and flag it to Sean. Nothing
  releases a stale claim, and **do not seize the files** — a stale claim is still a
  claim. Only Sean knows whether that session died or is merely mid-think.

> **This is cooperative collision avoidance, not mutual exclusion.** Claiming
> broadcasts; it does not lock. `claim()` writes its own file and reads no other
> lane, so two seats can both inspect an apparently clear target before either
> claim becomes visible. Re-check discovery after claiming and before editing. The
> race window is real and this protocol does not close it.

**Enforcement honesty (measured 2026-09-20).** For Cursor, nothing here is a hook —
this rule fires because an agent reads it. The deterministic layer exists but is
wired per-harness: `scripts/hooks/lane-session-start.mjs` runs at session start
from `.claude/settings.json` (Claude Code) and `.codebuddy/settings.json`
(WorkBuddy). **No harness hook has ever been observed firing on this machine**, so
treat automatic orientation as unproven on every surface and run `orientation`
yourself. Full spec: `docs/ai-workflow/references/AI-PAIR-CODING-PROTOCOL.md`,
Rule 67 in `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md` / `CODEBUDDY.md` / `GEMINI.md`.
